            <ArticleTitle>Lateral Antebrachial Cutaneous Nerve Entrapment After Shoulder Arthroscopy: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>889-94</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pmrj.2015.03.009</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1934-1482(15)00140-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve (LABCN) is a distal sensory branch of the musculocutaneous nerve that innervates the radial aspect of the forearm. Cases of LABCN injury from trauma and chronic compression have been reported. A case of musculocutaneous nerve injury after a biceps tenodesis has also been reported. This case report describes an LABCN injury and forearm pain after a biceps tenodesis procedure. Using a multifaceted diagnostic approach of electrodiagnostics and magnetic resonance neurography, the site of compression was appropriately localized. The patient ultimately achieved relief after a surgical decompression.</AbstractText>
